// ============================================================================
// lsp.bx — `burxt lsp` in Burxt: a language server over stdio. The counterpart of
// `src/rust-compiler/lsp.rs`.
//
// **This row was declared BLOCKED two versions ago and that was wrong.** v0.0.216's parity
// map said: *"the language server frames LSP messages over STDIN, and Burxt has no stdin. No
// builtin reads it, and `fread` is unreachable because a caller cannot make a pointer to
// writable memory."* Every sentence is a fact and the conclusion is false —
// `external function getchar() -> CInt touches input` was already declared in `lib/os.bx` and
// already in use. `tests/support/reads_stdin.bx` is the five-line program that proves it, and
// the rule it recorded is the one this file was written under:
//
//   > **Before writing "blocked", run the smallest program that would prove it.**
//
// So five things that looked like walls were each walked up to first, and every one of them
// gave way. They are the interesting content of this file and each is written down where it is
// answered:
//
//   1. *"`print` appends a newline, so exact protocol bytes are impossible."*  ->  §output.
//      `external function write(fd: CInt, text: String, count: Int)`, one line. It is also
//      unbuffered, which is what makes this a SERVER rather than a batch job: measured replying
//      in 1 ms with the client still holding stdin open, not at EOF.
//   2. *"the checker PRINTS its diagnostics, so it would corrupt the protocol stream."*  ->
//      §capture. `dup`/`dup2`/`fflush` turn that into a feature: the message this server
//      publishes is the compiler's own text, captured rather than re-worded here.
//   3. *"a diagnostic needs a span and `complain_at` keeps none."*  ->  §attribution. It keeps
//      no span, but it increments `problems` by exactly one and prints exactly one line, and the
//      check can be driven one ITEM at a time from outside. Measured: 221 of the 222 diagnostics
//      across `tests/fail/` land on exactly the token `check.bx` complained at, with no new
//      field anywhere.
//   4. *"Burxt cannot turn an arbitrary byte into a String, so a non-ASCII buffer cannot be
//      read."*  ->  §bytes. `ROADMAP-1.0.md` B2 is real and this is not blocked by it:
//      `write_bytes` then `read_file` is a byte-array-to-String that is exact for all 256
//      values, at two file operations per message. It also carries the `\uXXXX` decoding that
//      `os_byte_as_string`'s `?` cannot, surrogate pairs included.
//   5. *"a server runs for hours and a region is never reclaimed."*  ->  §serve. This one took
//      three measurements and the first two were wrong in the same direction — see there. The
//      answer is a region per TURN with the WHOLE turn inside it and the document store on disk.
//      Measured flat at 11 MB over 4000 keystrokes, on a 2.9 KB and a 13 KB buffer alike.
//
// **Nothing here is blocked.** Every limitation below is a stated behaviour with a number
// attached, which is a different thing from a wall, and the difference is the whole reason this
// file exists at all.
//
// ---- what agreement means for this file -------------------------------------------------
//
// **Less than for `diag.bx`, and the reason is not this file.** Driven over the same session,
// message for message, against `burxt lsp` and diffed as JSON — the numbers below are that
// comparison, not an impression of it.
//
// What agrees exactly: the framing, `initialize`'s capabilities, `hover` (byte for byte, value
// and range, including the rounding-contract note and types no program wrote down), the
// MethodNotFound reply, `shutdown`, the exit code for `exit` with and without it, and clearing a
// squiggle by publishing `[]`. On all 142 `tests/pass/` fixtures both servers publish the same
// thing — which is `[]` 142 times, and is the claim that matters most, because a server that
// invents a diagnostic in a file that compiles is worse than one that misses a real one.
//
// What does not: **the two compilers do not word their diagnostics alike and do not point at the
// same token.** For `let b: Bool = 2;` stage-0 says *"type mismatch in `let b`: declared Bool,
// but expression has type Int"* at the `2`, and the Burxt compiler says *"declared Bool, but the
// value is Int"* at the `b`. Across `tests/fail/` that is 13 of 269 fixtures with identical
// message text, 174 agreeing on the LINE, and 199 on the count. Of the 70 where even the count
// differs, all 70 are files the two COMPILERS disagree about — measured by asking each compiler's
// own CLI, and on all 411 fixtures this server's diagnostic count equals its own compiler's.
// **That divergence is older than this file and lives in `check.bx`'s messages.** A language
// server cannot close it, and a translation table here would hide it.
//
// ---- what is NOT here ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **One feature, and it is half of one.** `lsp.rs`'s `check_in_context` answers "what program
// does this file belong to" in two ways: a file WITH `use` lines is a program root, and a file
// without them might be a module some root assembles. The first half is here and is better than
// the Rust one — see §the program a buffer belongs to, where the buffer needs no splicing
// because only the imports are read from disk. The second half needs a directory listing to find
// the root, Burxt has no `read_dir`, and so a file that is `use`d by another is checked ALONE
// and reports every type its siblings declare as unknown. Named, not hidden.
//
// Go-to-definition is absent on both sides for the same reason: it needs the compiler to keep
// name resolution rather than only its result.
//
// ---- why no `lib/json.bx` --------------------------------------------------------------
//
// It exists and it works, and this file does not use it. Three reasons, in order of weight:
//
//   1. **The compiler modules do not depend on `lib/`.** `diag.bx` says so, `diag_harness.bx`
//      says so, and `diag.rs` hand-writes its own JSON escaping on the other side for the
//      same reason. `lsp.bx` sits in `src/burxt-compiler/`, so it inherits the rule. Pulling
//      `lib/json.bx` in would pull `lib/option.bx` and `lib/result.bx` with it and change what
//      the compiler IS — a program that carries the standard library.
//   2. **A server does not want a Value tree, it wants a path.** Every question this file asks
//      of a message is *"the string at `params.textDocument.uri`"*. With no closures and no
//      generic match, walking a parsed tree in Burxt is more code than scanning for a key —
//      and the failure mode of a scan is *"absent"*, which the protocol already has an answer
//      for, while the failure mode of a parser is a second thing that can be wrong.
//   3. It is 524 lines to reuse 120.
//
// The cost is stated plainly: the reader below is not a JSON parser. It does not validate, it
// does not build a tree, and it answers the wrong thing for input the protocol does not send.
// `json_escape` on the way OUT is `diag.bx`'s, because that one is shared with the renderer
// and must not fork.
// ============================================================================

use "ast.bx";
use "lexer.bx";
use "parser.bx";
use "check.bx";
use "emit.bx";
use "modules.bx";
use "diag.bx";

// `emit.bx` is in that list and `lsp.rs` needs no codegen — the front end is all a language
// server asks for. It is here because `class Unit` in `ast.bx` has fields typed `Slot` and
// `Declared`, which `emit.bx` declares: the one compilation unit holds the lexer's tokens, the
// parser's arena, the checker's bindings AND the backend's register state, so there is no way
// to take the front half. It costs compile time and a larger binary, nothing at runtime.
// Splitting `Unit` is a change to a file this file may not edit, so it is reported.
//
// `modules.bx` is there for `starts_with_at`, not for `load_program`. Writing a local copy of a
// three-line byte comparison would have been shorter than this comment, and wrong for the same
// reason `json_escape` is `diag.bx`'s: one implementation of a thing, in the module whose job it
// already is.

// ---- output ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `print` appends a newline and goes through `printf`, and a protocol frame is exact bytes
// with no newline — so neither the extra byte nor `printf`'s buffering is acceptable here.
// `write(2)` answers both: it takes a byte count and it is a syscall, so a reply is on the
// wire when the call returns rather than when a 4 KB buffer happens to fill. A client that
// waited for a full buffer would look like a server that hung.
//
// No effect declared, and that is consistent rather than an oversight: `print` declares none
// either, and there is no `output` effect in the language (`effect_name` lists files,
// commands, clock, input, network, model).
external function write(fd: CInt, text: String, count: Int) -> Int;

// One byte of standard input, or -1 at the end. The whole of the protocol's input side.
external function getchar() -> CInt touches input;

// ---- capture ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The front end PRINTS its diagnostics — `complain_at` in `check.bx`, the lexer in `lexer.bx`,
// the parser. On stdout. Which is the protocol stream.
//
// That looked like a reason this file could not exist, and it is the opposite: the checker's
// own text is exactly what a language server should publish, so the answer is to point fd 1
// at a scratch file for the duration of a check and read it back. Nothing in the compiler
// changes, and the published message is the compiler's, word for word, rather than a second
// wording maintained here that could drift from it.
//
// `fopen`/`fileno` rather than `open` with O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, because those three are
// numbers that differ between systems and a hard-coded 577 is a portability bug waiting for
// its platform.
external function dup(fd: CInt) -> CInt touches files;
external function dup2(from_fd: CInt, to_fd: CInt) -> CInt touches files;
external function fopen(path: String, mode: String) -> CPointer touches files;
external function fclose(stream: CPointer) -> CInt touches files;
external function fileno(stream: CPointer) -> CInt;
external function fflush(stream: CPointer) -> CInt touches files;
external function strstr(haystack: String, needle: String) -> CPointer;
external function getpid() -> CInt;

// A genuine C NULL, and no pointer arithmetic to get it: `strstr` answers NULL when the
// needle is not in the haystack, and an empty haystack never holds `x`.
//
// It is needed because `fflush(NULL)` is the only way to push `printf`'s buffer out from here.
// The buffer belongs to `stdout`, which is a C GLOBAL — Burxt can call a function but cannot
// name a variable in another language, so `fflush(stdout)` is unreachable and `fflush(NULL)`,
// which flushes every stream, is reachable. The broader hammer is the available one.
function c_null() -> CPointer {
    return strstr("", "x");
}

// A scratch path unique to this process, so two editors do not overwrite each other's.
function lsp_scratch(suffix: String) -> String {
    let pid: Int = getpid();
    return "/tmp/burxt-lsp-" + to_string(pid) + suffix;
}

// Point fd 1 at `path` and answer a dup of the real stdout, so it can be put back.
//
// **Nothing between `capture_begin` and `capture_end` may return early or exit.** fd 1 would
// stay redirected and the server would go silent while still reading requests, which is the
// worst failure shape available: alive, listening, mute. Every caller below is one straight
// line between the two for that reason.
function lsp_capture_begin(path: String) -> Int touches files {
    // Anything already pending on stdout belongs to the REAL stdout. Flushing first is what
    // stops it from landing in the capture file.
    fflush(c_null());
    let saved: Int = dup(1);
    let sink: CPointer = fopen(path, "w");
    if c_is_null(sink) {
        return saved;                                // no capture; the checker will be noisy
    }
    dup2(fileno(sink), 1);
    // The FILE* is deliberately leaked: closing it would close the fd 1 we just made, and
    // the process holds one at a time. `fflush(NULL)` below is what makes the bytes land.
    return saved;
}

function lsp_capture_end(saved: Int) -> Int touches files {
    fflush(c_null());                                // the checker's text into the file
    dup2(saved, 1);                                  // fd 1 is the protocol again
    return 0;
}

// ---- bytes -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **`ROADMAP-1.0.md` B2, answered rather than worked around.** Burxt has no byte-to-String:
// `os_byte_as_string` returns `?` for every byte >= 127, which is the row's whole content. A
// message body is arbitrary UTF-8 — a String literal in the program being edited may hold any of
// it — so building the body a byte at a time through a printable table would corrupt exactly the
// input this language claims to handle exactly, and would move every byte offset after it.
//
// `write_bytes` writes the low byte of every element of an `[Int]`, and `read_file` reads a file
// into a String. Together they ARE a byte-array-to-String, exact for all 256 values, at one write
// and one read — nothing beside the typecheck that follows. So B2 is not a wall for anything that
// can afford a file operation; it is a wall for a per-character conversion, which is a narrower
// claim than the row makes and worth taking back there.
//
// Every String that enters this server comes through here, which is also why `\uXXXX` decoding
// and a raw NUL are ordinary cases rather than special ones.
function lsp_bytes_as_text(bytes: [Int]) -> String touches files {
    let path: String = lsp_scratch(".msg");
    write_bytes(path, bytes);
    return read_file(path);
}

// ---- reading ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// True when `line` (a byte array) begins with `prefix`, ignoring case in neither: the header
// name is fixed by the protocol.
function lsp_line_starts_with(line: [Int], width: Int, prefix: String) -> Bool {
    if width < len(prefix) {
        return false;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(prefix) {
        if line[i] != byte_at(prefix, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// The digits after a header's colon, as an Int, or -1 when there are none.
function lsp_header_number(line: [Int], width: Int, from: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    while i < width && (line[i] == 32 || line[i] == 9) {
        i += 1;
    }
    let mutable value: Int = 0;
    let mutable digits: Int = 0;
    while i < width && line[i] >= 48 && line[i] <= 57 {
        value = value * 10 + (line[i] - 48);
        digits += 1;
        i += 1;
    }
    if digits == 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    return value;
}

// One `Content-Length`-framed message, as bytes. An EMPTY array means standard input ended
// and the client is gone.
//
// A zero-length body is indistinguishable from end of input here, and that is deliberate
// rather than sloppy: `Content-Length: 0` carries no JSON-RPC message, so there is nothing a
// caller could do with the difference.
function lsp_read_frame() -> [Int] touches input {
    let mutable length: Int = 0 - 1;
    let mutable ended: Bool = false;
    while !ended {
        let mutable line: [Int] = [];
        let mutable b: Int = getchar();
        while b >= 0 && b != 10 {
            push(line, b);
            b = getchar();
        }
        if b < 0 && len(line) == 0 {
            return [];                               // stdin closed between messages
        }
        // A CRLF client's `\r` belongs to the framing, not to the header.
        let mutable width: Int = len(line);
        if width > 0 && line[width - 1] == 13 {
            width -= 1;
        }
        if width == 0 {
            ended = true;                            // the blank line ends the headers
        } else {
            if lsp_line_starts_with(line, width, "Content-Length:") {
                length = lsp_header_number(line, width, len("Content-Length:"));
            }
            // Content-Type is the only other header the protocol defines and its value is
            // fixed, so reading it would change nothing.
            if b < 0 {
                return [];                           // headers cut off mid-way
            }
        }
    }
    if length < 0 {
        return [];                                   // no Content-Length: unframeable
    }
    let mutable body: [Int] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < length {
        let c: Int = getchar();
        if c < 0 {
            i = length;                              // truncated body; answer what arrived
        } else {
            push(body, c);
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return body;
}

// ---- JSON, read ------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// See the header for why this is here rather than `lib/json.bx`. It is a SCANNER: it finds the
// value of a named member of an object, and it knows just enough about JSON's shapes to skip
// past the ones it was not asked about. It never builds a tree.

pure function json_space(text: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = at;
    while i < len(text) {
        let b: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if b == 32 || b == 9 || b == 10 || b == 13 {
            i += 1;
        } else {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return i;
}

// One past the closing quote of the string starting at `at`. Escapes are skipped as pairs,
// which is the only thing a skipper needs to know about them: `\"` must not end the string.
pure function json_skip_string(text: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = at + 1;
    while i < len(text) {
        let b: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if b == 92 {
            i += 2;
        } else if b == 34 {
            return i + 1;
        } else {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return i;
}

// One past the end of whatever value starts at `at`. Objects and arrays are skipped by
// depth, with strings skipped whole so that a `\}` inside one cannot close anything.
pure function json_skip_value(text: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = json_space(text, at);
    if i >= len(text) {
        return i;
    }
    let first: Int = byte_at(text, i);
    if first == 34 {
        return json_skip_string(text, i);
    }
    if first == 123 || first == 91 {                 // '\{' or '['
        let mutable depth: Int = 0;
        while i < len(text) {
            let b: Int = byte_at(text, i);
            if b == 34 {
                i = json_skip_string(text, i);
            } else {
                if b == 123 || b == 91 {
                    depth += 1;
                }
                if b == 125 || b == 93 {             // '\}' or ']'
                    depth -= 1;
                    if depth == 0 {
                        return i + 1;
                    }
                }
                i += 1;
            }
        }
        return i;
    }
    // A number, `true`, `false` or `null`: it ends where the next structural byte begins.
    while i < len(text) {
        let b: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if b == 44 || b == 125 || b == 93 || b == 32 || b == 9 || b == 10 || b == 13 {
            return i;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return i;
}

// True when the string at `at` is exactly `name`. Compared RAW — no unescaping — which is
// right for a member name: the protocol's keys are ASCII letters and slashes, so an escape
// in one would mean a client that has already gone wrong somewhere else.
pure function json_string_is(text: String, at: Int, name: String) -> Bool {
    if at >= len(text) || byte_at(text, at) != 34 {
        return false;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = at + 1;
    let mutable k: Int = 0;
    while i < len(text) && byte_at(text, i) != 34 {
        if k >= len(name) || byte_at(text, i) != byte_at(name, k) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
    return k == len(name);
}

// Where the value of member `key` begins, in the object starting at `object_at`, or -1.
//
// Only the object's OWN members are looked at — a nested `"uri"` is skipped with its parent —
// which is what makes chained calls mean the same thing as `lsp.rs`'s
// `path(&["params", "textDocument", "uri"])` rather than "the first `uri` anywhere".
pure function json_member(text: String, object_at: Int, key: String) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = json_space(text, object_at);
    if i >= len(text) || byte_at(text, i) != 123 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    i += 1;
    while true {
        i = json_space(text, i);
        if i >= len(text) || byte_at(text, i) == 125 {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        if byte_at(text, i) != 34 {
            return 0 - 1;                            // not a member: malformed, and not fatal
        }
        let matched: Bool = json_string_is(text, i, key);
        let after_key: Int = json_space(text, json_skip_string(text, i));
        if after_key >= len(text) || byte_at(text, after_key) != 58 {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        let value: Int = json_space(text, after_key + 1);
        if matched {
            return value;
        }
        i = json_space(text, json_skip_value(text, value));
        if i < len(text) && byte_at(text, i) == 44 {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// The two- and three-deep forms, spelled out because Burxt has no varargs and a path array
// would need a loop that reads no better than this does.
pure function json_member2(text: String, at: Int, first: String, second: String) -> Int {
    let one: Int = json_member(text, at, first);
    if one < 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    return json_member(text, one, second);
}

pure function json_member3(text: String, at: Int, first: String, second: String,
        third: String) -> Int {
    let two: Int = json_member2(text, at, first, second);
    if two < 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    return json_member(text, two, third);
}

// Where the LAST element of the array at `at` begins, or -1. `contentChanges` is why: under
// full sync the last change carries the whole document.
pure function json_array_last(text: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = json_space(text, at);
    if i >= len(text) || byte_at(text, i) != 91 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    i += 1;
    let mutable found: Int = 0 - 1;
    while true {
        i = json_space(text, i);
        if i >= len(text) || byte_at(text, i) == 93 {
            return found;
        }
        found = i;
        i = json_space(text, json_skip_value(text, i));
        if i < len(text) && byte_at(text, i) == 44 {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return found;
}

// Four hex digits as an Int, or -1.
pure function json_hex4(text: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable value: Int = 0;
    let mutable k: Int = 0;
    while k < 4 {
        if at + k >= len(text) {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        let b: Int = byte_at(text, at + k);
        let mutable d: Int = 0 - 1;
        if b >= 48 && b <= 57 {
            d = b - 48;
        } else if b >= 97 && b <= 102 {
            d = b - 87;
        } else if b >= 65 && b <= 70 {
            d = b - 55;
        }
        if d < 0 {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        value = value * 16 + d;
        k += 1;
    }
    return value;
}

// The string value at `at`, unescaped, as BYTES. `[]` when `at` does not hold a string, which
// the callers treat as "absent" — a distinction the protocol never needs, because no LSP field
// this file reads is meaningfully the empty string.
//
// **Bytes and not a String, and the reason is a measured leak rather than taste.** The first
// version built a String a byte at a time in 4096-byte chunks — `lib/os.bx`'s pattern, whose
// comment says chunking is there "because `text += one_byte` copies the whole String for every
// byte". That is true and it is only half the cost. `chunk += one_byte` ALLOCATES a new String
// every byte, and a region allocator never reuses one, so a 2.9 KB document costs
// sum(1..2900) ~= 4.2 MB of region per read. Measured: 200 `didChange` messages on
// `examples/tour.bx` peaked at 756 MB and the server died of region exhaustion at about 250
// keystrokes — *"region memory exhausted"*, in a program whose per-turn region was working
// perfectly. The turn was cheap; READING THE MESSAGE was not, and it happens outside the turn
// because the document has to outlive it.
//
// `push` on an `[Int]` doubles, so this is O(n) allocation instead of O(n^2), and the array
// becomes a String once through the same `write_bytes`/`read_file` round trip the message body
// already uses.
//
// **It also closes the `\uXXXX` limitation** rather than working around it. The earlier version
// could not spell a byte above 0x7F and answered `?`, shifting every byte offset after it. Bytes
// are bytes, so the UTF-8 encoding below is exact, surrogate pairs included — which is how an
// emoji in a document arrives over the wire, and is what `json.rs` does on the other side. The
// two readers now agree on every input either one can be handed.
function json_string_bytes(text: String, at: Int) -> [Int] {
    let mutable out: [Int] = [];
    if at < 0 || at >= len(text) || byte_at(text, at) != 34 {
        return out;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = at + 1;
    while i < len(text) {
        let b: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if b == 34 {
            return out;
        }
        if b == 92 {
            let e: Int = byte_at(text, i + 1);
            if e == 110 {
                push(out, 10);
            } else if e == 116 {
                push(out, 9);
            } else if e == 114 {
                push(out, 13);
            } else if e == 98 {
                push(out, 8);
            } else if e == 102 {
                push(out, 12);
            } else if e == 117 {
                let mutable code: Int = json_hex4(text, i + 2);
                i += 4;
                // A high surrogate is half a codepoint; the low one follows as a second `\u`.
                if code >= 55296 && code < 56320 && byte_at(text, i + 2) == 92
                        && byte_at(text, i + 3) == 117 {
                    let low: Int = json_hex4(text, i + 4);
                    if low >= 56320 && low < 57344 {
                        code = 65536 + shift_left(code - 55296, 10) + (low - 56320);
                        i += 6;
                    }
                }
                push_utf8(out, code);
            } else {
                push(out, byte_at(text, i + 1));     // `\"`, `\\`, `\/` are themselves
            }
            i += 2;
        } else {
            push(out, b);
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return out;
}

// One codepoint as UTF-8, appended. Neither this nor `json_string_bytes` is `pure`, and the
// reason is `spec/N2` Decision 5 working as designed: `mutable out: [Int]` says this call changes
// what the caller passed, and "changes nothing" is the same promise `pure` makes. A pure
// function that appends is a contradiction, so the word goes rather than the append.
//
// The four widths, spelled out; a value outside Unicode
// becomes U+FFFD rather than a byte sequence no decoder would accept, because a client that
// sends one has already gone wrong and a server's job is to keep serving.
function push_utf8(mutable out: [Int], code: Int) -> Int {
    if code < 0 || code > 1114111 {
        push(out, 239);
        push(out, 191);
        push(out, 189);
        return 3;
    }
    if code < 128 {
        push(out, code);
        return 1;
    }
    if code < 2048 {
        push(out, bit_or(192, divide_floor(code, 64)));
        push(out, bit_or(128, remainder(code, 64)));
        return 2;
    }
    if code < 65536 {
        push(out, bit_or(224, divide_floor(code, 4096)));
        push(out, bit_or(128, remainder(divide_floor(code, 64), 64)));
        push(out, bit_or(128, remainder(code, 64)));
        return 3;
    }
    push(out, bit_or(240, divide_floor(code, 262144)));
    push(out, bit_or(128, remainder(divide_floor(code, 4096), 64)));
    push(out, bit_or(128, remainder(divide_floor(code, 64), 64)));
    push(out, bit_or(128, remainder(code, 64)));
    return 4;
}

// The string value at `at`, as a String. One file round trip, for the reason above.
function json_string_at(text: String, at: Int) -> String touches files {
    return lsp_bytes_as_text(json_string_bytes(text, at));
}

// A non-negative integer at `at`, or -1. Protocol positions are the only numbers read, and a
// negative line has no meaning — so "not a number" and "negative" answer the same thing.
pure function json_int_at(text: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    if at < 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = json_space(text, at);
    let mutable value: Int = 0;
    let mutable digits: Int = 0;
    while i < len(text) && byte_at(text, i) >= 48 && byte_at(text, i) <= 57 {
        value = value * 10 + (byte_at(text, i) - 48);
        digits += 1;
        i += 1;
    }
    if digits == 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    return value;
}

// The `id` of a request, VERBATIM — the bytes as the client wrote them, because JSON-RPC
// allows a string or a number and a reply must echo the same one back. Reading it as an Int
// and printing it again would turn `"id":"7"` into `"id":7`, which a strict client is right
// to reject. `""` when there is no id, which is what distinguishes a notification.
pure function json_raw_at(text: String, at: Int) -> String {
    if at < 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let stop: Int = json_skip_value(text, at);
    let from: Int = json_space(text, at);
    if stop <= from {
        return "";
    }
    return substring(text, from, stop - from);
}

// ---- JSON, write -----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Built as text rather than through a Value type, which is the same trade as the reader and
// one more reason besides: `diag.bx` already writes its LSP positions this way, and two ways
// of spelling one object is how the two halves of an editor integration drift apart.
//
// A literal `\{` and `\}` are escaped in every string below, because a bare `{` opens an
// interpolation in Burxt. JSON-RPC is nothing but braces, so this is the first error anyone
// writing this file makes, and it is worth a line to say so.

function lsp_send(body: String) -> Int {
    // Content-Length counts BYTES. A message with a non-ASCII identifier in it would
    // otherwise be truncated at the client, which is `lsp.rs`'s note and the same `len` here:
    // `len` on a Burxt String is its byte count, so this is right by construction rather than
    // by care.
    let header: String = "Content-Length: " + to_string(len(body)) + "\r\n\r\n";
    write(1, header, len(header));
    write(1, body, len(body));
    return 0;
}

function lsp_respond(id: String, result: String) -> Int {
    return lsp_send("\{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":" + id + ",\"result\":" + result + "\}");
}

// -32601 is MethodNotFound. An unknown REQUEST must be answered or the client waits forever;
// an unknown notification is ignored. The presence of an `id` is the whole difference.
function lsp_method_not_found(id: String, method: String) -> Int {
    return lsp_send("\{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":" + id + ",\"error\":\{\"code\":-32601,"
            + "\"message\":" + json_escape("unsupported method `" + method + "`") + "\}\}");
}

function lsp_publish_message(uri: String, diagnostics: String) -> Int {
    return lsp_send("\{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"textDocument/publishDiagnostics\","
                + "\"params\":\{\"uri\":" + json_escape(uri) + ",\"diagnostics\":" + diagnostics
            + "\}\}");
}

// 1 = Full sync: the client sends the whole document on every change. Incremental sync would
// need a text-edit applier to be correct, and the correctness of the buffer is the one thing
// a server cannot get wrong — `lsp.rs` makes the same call in the same words.
//
// `serverInfo` names the server and does NOT state a version, and the difference from
// `lsp.rs` is deliberate. There it is `env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION")`, read from the file that
// defines the release; Burxt has no such constant, and a version hand-copied into a string
// here would be wrong one release after it was written. An absent field is honest; a stale one
// is a lie a client would repeat in its logs.
function lsp_initialize_result() -> String {
    return "\{\"capabilities\":\{\"textDocumentSync\":1,\"hoverProvider\":true\},"
            + "\"serverInfo\":\{\"name\":\"burxt-lsp\"\}\}";
}

// ---- positions -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The byte offset of a 0-based (line, character) protocol position.
//
// Characters are counted in CODEPOINTS, matching `LineIndex::offset_of` in `diag.rs`. The
// protocol actually specifies UTF-16 code units; both servers count codepoints, so the two
// AGREE with each other and both differ from the specification above the BMP. That is one
// shared bug rather than two behaviours, which is the better place for it to be until
// `ROADMAP-1.0.md` A5 lands — and it is stated here so the next reader does not think this
// side chose it independently.
pure function lsp_offset_of(src: String, line: Int, character: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    let mutable rows: Int = 0;
    while rows < line && at < len(src) {
        if byte_at(src, at) == 10 {
            rows += 1;
        }
        at += 1;
    }
    if rows < line {
        return len(src);                             // past the last line
    }
    let mutable seen: Int = 0;
    while at < len(src) && byte_at(src, at) != 10 && seen < character {
        at += 1;
        // Every byte that is not a UTF-8 continuation byte begins a codepoint. `diag.bx`'s
        // header derives this; the same rule read forwards is a cursor's byte offset.
        while at < len(src) && bit_and(byte_at(src, at), 192) == 128 {
            at += 1;
        }
        seen += 1;
    }
    return at;
}

// One 0-based protocol position, as JSON. `diag.bx` counts lines and columns from 1 for
// people; the protocol counts from 0, and this is the one place the subtraction happens.
function lsp_position(src: String, offset: Int) -> String {
    return "\{\"line\":" + to_string(line_number_at(src, offset) - 1)
            + ",\"character\":" + to_string(column_at(src, offset) - 1) + "\}";
}

function lsp_range(src: String, from: Int, to: Int) -> String {
    return "\{\"start\":" + lsp_position(src, from) + ",\"end\":" + lsp_position(src, to) + "\}";
}

// ---- the front end ---------------------------------------------------------------------

// **A fresh `Unit` is spelled out at each of the two places that needs one**, and that is a
// real cost worth naming rather than a slip. A `new_unit()` helper was written first and the
// compiler refused it: *"cannot return Unit, because its storage lives in a region and would
// not outlive it"* — which is the region rule doing its job, since the Unit would be built in
// the helper's frame. `allocates` does not lift it; a class simply cannot be returned.
//
// So the sixty-field literal now appears three times in the tree: twice below and once in
// `main.bx`. The saving grace is that duplication here is LOUD — a new field on `Unit` makes
// every copy fail to compile, so this cannot rot silently, only noisily. The fix is a language
// one (a constructor that allocates in the caller's region, which is what `allocates` promises
// for values but not for classes) and it is reported rather than worked around.

// ---- the program a buffer belongs to --------------------------------------------------
//
// **A file is not always a program.** `src/burxt-compiler/check.bx` is one of six modules
// `main.bx` assembles; `tests/pass/map_library.bx` opens with `use "lib/map.bx";`. Checking
// either one as an isolated buffer is wrong in two different ways, and both were measured
// before this section existed:
//
//   * a file WITH imports had its `use` lines lexed as ordinary statements, so eleven of the
//     pass fixtures published a parse error the compiler does not make — the editor showing a
//     mistake in a file that compiles;
//   * a file that IS a module reports every type declared in a sibling as unknown.
//
// The first is fixed here and the second is not, and the difference is a directory listing.
// `lsp.rs` finds the root of a module by reading the file's own directory and its ancestors;
// Burxt has no `read_dir`, so that half stays missing and is named in the header.
//
// **The root case needs no splicing**, which is where this improves on `lsp.rs` rather than
// merely matching it. There, the program is loaded from disk and the editor's text is spliced
// over the file's own span, with a source map to keep later files' offsets right. Here only the
// IMPORTS are read from disk and the buffer is appended after them — so the root is never read
// from disk at all, and the editor's text is authoritative by construction instead of by
// arithmetic.
//
// `Loader` in `modules.bx` would do the walk, and it is not used: `read_file` on a path that
// does not exist ENDS THE PROCESS (`burxt runtime error: cannot open file for reading`), and
// `use "not-written-yet.bx";` is a thing a person types every day. A server that dies there is
// the failure `lsp.rs` calls the worst one available. So the walk is repeated with one extra
// line in it — an existence check — and `missing` is how the caller learns to fall back.

// Whether a path can be opened for reading. The only guard there is: `read_file` does not
// answer a failure, it ends the program.
function lsp_readable(path: String) -> Bool touches files {
    let f: CPointer = fopen(path, "r");
    if c_is_null(f) {
        return false;
    }
    fclose(f);
    return true;
}

// The `file://` path a uri names, or `""`. Anything else — `untitled:`, a remote scheme — has
// no path, and a buffer with no path is checked alone, which is correct rather than a gap: it
// has no siblings on this disk to check it against.
function lsp_path_of(uri: String) -> String {
    if !starts_with_at(uri, 0, "file://", len(uri)) {
        return "";
    }
    return substring(uri, len("file://"), len(uri) - len("file://"));
}

// Every file a buffer imports, transitively, in dependency order, each once — `Loader.load`'s
// walk with an existence check added. `missing` is the count of `use` targets that are not
// there; the caller treats any as "do not splice", because half a program is worse than no
// program: the missing half's types would come back as unknown and silence the rules that
// mention them.
class Imports { seen: [String], buffer: String, missing: Int }

function (mutable self: Imports) gather(directory: String, text: String) -> Int touches files {
    let width: Int = len(text);
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    let mutable in_header: Bool = true;
    while at < width && in_header {
        let stop: Int = end_of_line(text, at, width);
        let begin: Int = first_non_space(text, at, stop);
        if begin >= stop || starts_with_at(text, begin, "//", stop) {
            at = stop + 1;
        } else {
            let named: String = import_on_line(text, at, stop);
            if len(named) == 0 {
                in_header = false;
            } else {
                self.take(directory + named);
                at = stop + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return len(self.seen);
}

function (mutable self: Imports) take(path: String) -> Int touches files {
    for already in self.seen {
        if already == path {
            return 0;                                // read once; a cycle is harmless
        }
    }
    push(self.seen, path);
    if !lsp_readable(path) {
        self.missing += 1;
        return 0;
    }
    let text: String = read_file(path);
    // Its own imports FIRST, so a declaration is in the buffer before whatever uses it.
    self.gather(directory_of(path), text);
    if len(self.buffer) > 0 {
        self.buffer += "\n";                         // between files, never after the last
    }
    self.buffer += blank_imports(text);
    return 1;
}

// The text to check, and how far into it the buffer starts.
//
// Answers the buffer's own blanked text with a shift of 0 whenever there is nothing to prepend
// — no imports, no path, or an import that is not on disk yet. Every caller therefore has one
// code path, and the multi-file case is a longer prefix rather than a different mode.
function lsp_program_prefix(uri: String, src: String) -> String touches files {
    let blanked: String = blank_imports(src);
    if blanked == src {
        return "";                                   // no `use` lines: the file is its own program
    }
    let path: String = lsp_path_of(uri);
    if len(path) == 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let mutable imports: Imports = Imports { seen: [], buffer: "", missing: 0 };
    imports.gather(directory_of(path), src);
    if imports.missing > 0 || len(imports.buffer) == 0 {
        return "";
    }
    return imports.buffer + "\n";
}

// ---- attribution -----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **The interesting problem in this file, and the third wall that was not one.**
//
// `complain_at` in `check.bx` does this:
//
//     print("error: " + message + " (at `" + substring(src, t.start, t.length) + "`)");
//     self.problems += 1;
//
// It HAS the token. It keeps nothing. So a diagnostic's span looked unobtainable without a
// new field on `Unit` and a new line in `check.bx` — a file this file may not edit.
//
// Three facts recover it exactly, none of them new:
//
//   1. one call prints exactly one line AND increments `problems` by exactly one, so the
//      k-th `error:` line in the capture is the k-th increment;
//   2. `check_bodies` is a plain loop over `items`, so the same loop can be written HERE and
//      `problems` read before and after each item — which says how many of those lines each
//      top-level item produced;
//   3. the line names the offending token's TEXT, and an item's token range is bounded by the
//      next item's first token.
//
// So: the item gives the region, the printed text picks the token inside it. Where a name
// occurs once in its item — which is the ordinary case, and is the case for every shape the
// editor sees most — the span is the exact token `complain_at` was given. Where it occurs
// more than once the FIRST occurrence in the item is used, and successive diagnostics naming
// the same text in the same item advance through the occurrences. That can land one
// occurrence off inside a single statement; it cannot land in the wrong statement, which is
// the error that would matter.
//
// What this does NOT recover: a complaint from `collect_declarations`, `check_applications`
// or `infer_allocates`, which run before any item is walked. Those get the token their text
// names if it is unique in the WHOLE file, and the start of the file otherwise. `lsp.rs`
// points at them exactly. Stated, not hidden.
//
// The honest alternative is three fields on `Unit` and one line in `complain_at`, which would
// make all of this eleven lines instead of a hundred and would give the exact span every
// time. It is reported rather than made.

// None of the six functions below is `pure`, and each of them could be: they read text and
// answer. The reason is `starts_with_at` in `modules.bx`, which is not declared `pure` — and
// `spec/N2` Decision 5 is right to refuse the guarantee on top of a function that does not make
// it. One word in `modules.bx` would let all six carry it; that file is not this file's to edit,
// so it is reported.
//
// How many bytes of the line at `at` are the prefix that marks it a diagnostic, or -1 when it
// is not one.
//
// **Two prefixes, and finding the second one cost a real bug.** The lexer and the checker print
// `error: `; the parser prints `parse error: `. The first version of this file matched only
// `error: `, so `let x: Int = 1` with the semicolon missing published an EMPTY diagnostics array
// — a buffer that does not parse, and the editor showing nothing wrong with it. Caught by
// driving both servers over the same session and diffing, which is the only reason it was
// caught at all: every unit test of the framing passed.
//
// So this function is the seam, and it is written as a list rather than as a `starts_with`
// because a third prefix appearing in the front end must be a compile-time-visible line here,
// not a silent miss. This is the check, and it is worth RUNNING rather than trusting the numbers
// under it:
//
//     grep -o 'print("[a-z ]*' src/burxt-compiler/{lexer,parser,check}.bx
//
// **16 `error` and 2 `parse error` as of v0.0.233, re-measured.** The line above used to name
// `types.bx`, which v0.0.233 split into `ast.bx` and `lexer.bx` — so it had become a command
// pointing at a path that does not exist, and `grep` on a missing file prints nothing, which a
// reader skimming would take for *"no new prefixes, nothing to do"*. A stale comment is a
// nuisance; **a stale INSTRUMENT answers the question wrongly and looks like an answer.**
//
// That is `spec/A7.0-NAMING.md` §9, and this file was its victim rather than its witness: the same
// rename also turned "the lexer in `types.bx`" into "the lexer in `ast.bx`" in the §capture note
// above, which is the WRONG HALF — the lexer's fifteen error prints are all in `lexer.bx` and
// `ast.bx` has none. Both were mechanically correct substitutions and both were false.
function lsp_diagnostic_prefix(printed: String, at: Int, stop: Int) -> Int {
    if starts_with_at(printed, at, "error: ", stop) {
        return len("error: ");
    }
    if starts_with_at(printed, at, "parse error: ", stop) {
        return len("parse error: ");
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// Where the k-th diagnostic line starts, or -1. The capture holds one line per diagnostic;
// anything else the front end prints is skipped by the prefix test.
function lsp_error_line(printed: String, which: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable seen: Int = 0;
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    while at < len(printed) {
        let stop: Int = line_end_at(printed, at);
        if lsp_diagnostic_prefix(printed, at, stop) >= 0 {
            if seen == which {
                return at;
            }
            seen += 1;
        }
        at = stop + 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

function lsp_count_error_lines(printed: String) -> Int {
    let mutable seen: Int = 0;
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    while at < len(printed) {
        let stop: Int = line_end_at(printed, at);
        if lsp_diagnostic_prefix(printed, at, stop) >= 0 {
            seen += 1;
        }
        at = stop + 1;
    }
    return seen;
}

// The message on the `error: ` line at `at`, without the ` (at `x`)` suffix that names the
// token. The suffix is `complain_at`'s way of pointing at source in a terminal; in an editor
// the RANGE points, so repeating the token in the text would be noise the Rust server does
// not produce either.
function lsp_message_of(printed: String, at: Int) -> String {
    let stop: Int = line_end_at(printed, at);
    let from: Int = at + lsp_diagnostic_prefix(printed, at, stop);
    let cut: Int = lsp_rfind(printed, from, stop, " (at `");
    if cut < 0 {
        return substring(printed, from, stop - from);
    }
    return substring(printed, from, cut - from);
}

// The token text named by the ` (at `x`)` suffix, or `""` when the line has none — the lexer
// and the parser print messages of that shape.
function lsp_token_text_of(printed: String, at: Int) -> String {
    let stop: Int = line_end_at(printed, at);
    let cut: Int = lsp_rfind(printed, at, stop, " (at `");
    if cut < 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let from: Int = cut + len(" (at `");
    // The suffix ends with "`)" and nothing else may follow it on the line.
    if stop - from < 2 {
        return "";
    }
    return substring(printed, from, stop - from - 2);
}

// The LAST occurrence of `needle` in `[from, stop)`, or -1. Last rather than first because a
// message may itself contain a backtick — `` `foo` touches files `` is one — and it is the
// suffix that has to be found.
function lsp_rfind(text: String, from: Int, stop: Int, needle: String) -> Int {
    let mutable found: Int = 0 - 1;
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    while i + len(needle) <= stop {
        if starts_with_at(text, i, needle, stop) {
            found = i;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return found;
}

// ---- diagnostics -----------------------------------------------------------------------

// Every problem in `src`, as the `diagnostics` array of a `publishDiagnostics` notification.
// `[]` when the buffer is clean — which matters as much as publishing an error, because it is
// what CLEARS the squiggle when the code becomes valid.
//
// `touches files` because of the capture file and the scratch write, not because it looks at
// the program's own imports: it does not. A `use`d module is checked ALONE here, so a file
// that names a type declared in a sibling reports it as unknown. `lsp.rs` avoids that by
// listing the directory to find the program the file belongs to, and Burxt has no directory
// listing. That is the one whole feature of `lsp.rs` missing here.
function lsp_diagnostics(uri: String, buffer: String) -> String touches files {
    // Everything below reads `src`, which is the buffer with any imported files in front of it
    // and its own `use` lines blanked out. `shift` is where the buffer begins inside it: a span
    // below that came from a sibling file and is not this buffer's to report, and a span above
    // it is `shift` bytes further along than the editor thinks.
    let prefix: String = lsp_program_prefix(uri, buffer);
    let shift: Int = len(prefix);
    let src: String = prefix + blank_imports(buffer);
    // Recorded per PROBLEM, in the order the front end produces them. See §attribution.
    let mutable item_from: [Int] = [];               // first token of the item that complained
    let mutable item_to: [Int] = [];                 // one past its last token
    let mutable unit: Unit = Unit { pending_public: 0, package_names: [], package_starts: [], package_ends: [], private_names: [], private_owners: [], tokens: [], keywords: [], nodes: [], children: [], nested_children: [], items: [], bindings: [], consts: [], functions: [], stack: [], methods: [], types: [], params_in_scope: [], generics: [], type_parameters: [], type_param_bounds: [], pending_bounds: [], current_generic: 0 - 1, current_method: 0 - 1, probing: 0, probe_changes: 0, grow_parameters: [], grow_self: [], relay_parameters: [], relay_self: [], relay_method_parameters: [], relay_aliases: [], relay_ceiling: 0, type_bindings: [], measure_clause: 0 - 1, measure_slot: 0, measure_parameters: 0 - 1, measure_owner: "", old_nodes: [], old_slots: [], ensures_start: 0, ensures_count: 0, ensures_owner: "", bracket_clauses: [], clause_subjects: [], it_token: 0 - 1, it_is_result: 0, used_it: 0, it_seen: 0, result_type_node: 0 - 1, result_slot: 0 - 1, returns_node: 0 - 1, walked_types: [], type_binding_floor: 0, binding_floor: 0, allowed_effects: 63, effects_owner: "", function_buckets: [], function_chain: [], instances: [], instance_arguments: [], impls: [], impl_traits: [], typed: [], region_depth: 0, region_token: 0 - 1, in_caller_region: 0, in_pure: 0, loop_depth: 0, in_contract: 0, in_ensures: 0, current_function: -1, chunks: [], pending: "", globals: "", global_chunks: [], sret: "", sret_cells: 0, function_entry: "", body_chunks: [], body_pending: "", next_register: 0, next_slot: 0, next_label: 0, slots: [], strings: [], declared: [], loop_condition_labels: [], loop_end_labels: [], region_marks: [], loop_region_depths: [], block_release: [], placing: 0, rel_frames: [], rel_names: [], in_function: 0, depth: 0, param_depth: 0 - 1, problems: 0, diagnose: 0, problem_start: 0 - 1, problem_length: 0, problem_message: "", position: 0, token_end: 0, no_record_literal: 0, in_extern_signature: 0, range_bounds: 0, in_multiply: 0, print_to_stderr: 0, int_type_node: 0 - 1, errors: 0, type_of_calls: 0, find_binding_calls: 0, find_function_calls: 0, show_type_calls: 0, expr_allocates_calls: 0, type_at_calls: 0 };
    unit.install_keywords();

    let path: String = lsp_scratch(".out");
    let saved: Int = lsp_capture_begin(path);
    // ---- nothing from here to `lsp_capture_end` may return or exit ----
    unit.run(src);
    // Lexer complaints come before any item exists and name no token, so the file is all the
    // region there is for them.
    let mutable accounted: Int = 0;
    while accounted < unit.errors {
        push(item_from, 0);
        push(item_to, len(unit.tokens));
        accounted += 1;
    }
    let mutable parsing: Bool = true;
    while parsing {
        if unit.done() {
            parsing = false;
        } else {
            let item: Int = unit.parse_item(src);
            if item < 0 {
                parsing = false;
            } else {
                push(unit.items, item);
            }
        }
    }
    // A parse error DOES have a place: `position` is the token the parser stopped at. This is
    // the one span here that is exact without any matching — measured against the Rust server,
    // the range and the message both agree exactly for `expected \`;\`, found \`print\`` — and it
    // is also the only diagnostic either server reports ALONE: recovering a token stream is its
    // own design question on both sides.
    //
    // `position` past the last token means "expected ... found the end of the file", and the
    // place for that is the end of the last token there IS, not token zero. `-1` marks it, and
    // the renderer below turns it into a zero-width span at the end of the source, which is
    // where `lsp.rs` puts it too.
    while accounted < unit.errors {
        if unit.position < len(unit.tokens) {
            push(item_from, unit.position);
            push(item_to, unit.position + 1);
        } else {
            push(item_from, 0 - 1);
            push(item_to, 0 - 1);
        }
        accounted += 1;
    }

    if unit.errors == 0 {
        unit.collect_declarations(src);
        unit.check_applications(src);
        unit.infer_allocates(src);
        // Declaration-time complaints belong to no item. The whole token range is their
        // region, so a unique name still lands exactly and a repeated one lands at the file.
        let mutable declared: Int = unit.problems;
        let mutable before_items: Int = 0;
        while before_items < declared {
            push(item_from, 0);
            push(item_to, len(unit.tokens));
            before_items += 1;
        }
        // `check_bodies`, written here so `problems` can be read between items. The three arms
        // are that function's, unchanged — a fourth kind appearing there and not here would
        // mean an item nobody checks, which is why this mirrors it line for line.
        let mutable i: Int = 0;
        while i < len(unit.items) {
            let index: Int = unit.items[i];
            let before: Int = unit.problems;
            let item: Node = unit.nodes[index];
            if item.kind == 80 || item.kind == 81 {
                unit.check_body(src, index);
            }
            if item.kind == 85 {
                unit.check_impl(src, index);
                let mutable k: Int = 0;
                while k < item.b {
                    unit.check_body(src, unit.children[item.a + k]);
                    k += 1;
                }
            }
            // `|| == 74` rather than `<= 74`: 73 is `const`, an ITEM. See ast.bx kind 73.
            if (item.kind >= 60 && item.kind <= 72) || item.kind == 74 {
                unit.check_stmt_node(src, index, unknown_type());
            }
            let from_token: Int = item.token;
            let mutable to_token: Int = len(unit.tokens);
            if i + 1 < len(unit.items) {
                to_token = unit.nodes[unit.items[i + 1]].token;
            }
            let mutable mine: Int = unit.problems - before;
            while mine > 0 {
                push(item_from, from_token);
                push(item_to, to_token);
                mine -= 1;
            }
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    lsp_capture_end(saved);
    // ---- the capture window ends here; printing is safe again ----

    let printed: String = read_file(path);
    let total: Int = lsp_count_error_lines(printed);
    let mutable out: String = "[";
    let mutable which: Int = 0;
    let mutable published: Int = 0;
    while which < total {
        let line_at: Int = lsp_error_line(printed, which);
        let message: String = lsp_message_of(printed, line_at);
        let wanted: String = lsp_token_text_of(printed, line_at);
        // Which region this diagnostic came from. A count mismatch between lines and recorded
        // regions cannot happen for a complaint that went through `complain_at`, but a future
        // `print` in the front end that begins with `error: ` would cause one — so it falls
        // back to the whole file rather than reading past the end of the arrays.
        let mutable from_token: Int = 0;
        let mutable to_token: Int = len(unit.tokens);
        if which < len(item_from) {
            from_token = item_from[which];
            to_token = item_to[which];
        }
        // How many earlier diagnostics in the SAME region already claimed this text, so two
        // complaints about two occurrences of one name advance rather than pile up.
        let skip: Int = lsp_earlier_claims(printed, which, item_from, item_to, from_token);
        let mutable token: Int = unit.lsp_token_named(src, wanted, from_token, to_token, skip);
        // **`skip` is a preference, not a requirement, and getting that wrong cost real spans.**
        // The rule above assumes two complaints naming one text in one item are about two
        // OCCURRENCES of it. Often they are about the SAME token twice — `pair(1, "two")` draws
        // both *"argument 2 does not fit T"* and *"argument 2 should be T"*, and the item holds
        // one `pair`. The first version then found nothing for the second complaint and fell
        // back to underlining the item's first token, which measured as five wrong spans out of
        // 222 across `tests/fail/`. Retrying at the first occurrence fixes all five: a repeated
        // complaint about one token is commoner than one complaint about each of two.
        if token < 0 && skip > 0 {
            token = unit.lsp_token_named(src, wanted, from_token, to_token, 0);
        }
        let mutable span_start: Int = 0;
        let mutable span_end: Int = 0;
        if token >= 0 {
            span_start = unit.tokens[token].start;
            span_end = span_start + unit.tokens[token].length;
        } else if from_token < 0 {
            // End of the file: a zero-width span just past the last token, so the editor puts
            // the cursor where the missing `;` should have gone rather than on line 1.
            if len(unit.tokens) > 0 {
                let last: Token = unit.tokens[len(unit.tokens) - 1];
                span_start = last.start + last.length;
                span_end = span_start;
            }
        } else if from_token < len(unit.tokens) {
            // No named token to find: underline the region's first token, which for a
            // statement is where the statement begins.
            span_start = unit.tokens[from_token].start;
            span_end = span_start + unit.tokens[from_token].length;
        }
        // A problem in a sibling file is dropped: it is real, and it is not this document's,
        // and publishing it here would put a squiggle on whatever line of THIS file happens to
        // share the offset. `lsp.rs` filters the same way for the same reason.
        if span_start >= shift {
            if published > 0 {
                out += ",";
            }
            published += 1;
            // `severity` 1 is Error. Burxt has no warnings: every diagnostic it can produce is
            // a refusal to compile, which is `lsp.rs`'s note and the same constant.
            //
            // Positions are rendered against the BUFFER, so the offsets come back down by
            // `shift` — and against `blank_imports(buffer)` rather than `buffer` itself, which
            // is the same bytes at the same offsets by construction.
            out += "\{\"range\":" + lsp_range(blank_imports(buffer), span_start - shift,
                    span_end - shift)
                    + ",\"severity\":1,\"source\":\"burxt\",\"message\":" + json_escape(message) + "\}";
        }
        which += 1;
    }
    return out + "]";
}

// How many diagnostics before `which` named the same token text inside the same region.
function lsp_earlier_claims(printed: String, which: Int, item_from: [Int], item_to: [Int],
        from_token: Int) -> Int {
    let wanted: String = lsp_token_text_of(printed, lsp_error_line(printed, which));
    if len(wanted) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let mutable seen: Int = 0;
    let mutable k: Int = 0;
    while k < which {
        if k < len(item_from) && item_from[k] == from_token {
            if lsp_token_text_of(printed, lsp_error_line(printed, k)) == wanted {
                seen += 1;
            }
        }
        k += 1;
    }
    return seen;
}

// The index of the `skip`-th token in `[from, to)` whose text is exactly `wanted`, or -1.
function (self: Unit) lsp_token_named(src: String, wanted: String, from: Int, to: Int,
        skip: Int) -> Int {
    if len(wanted) == 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let mutable passed: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    let mutable stop: Int = to;
    if stop > len(self.tokens) {
        stop = len(self.tokens);
    }
    while i < stop {
        let t: Token = self.tokens[i];
        if t.length == len(wanted) && substring(src, t.start, t.length) == wanted {
            if passed == skip {
                return i;
            }
            passed += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// ---- hover -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Hover earns its place more in Burxt than in most languages: a `Decimal<2, RoundHalfEven>`
// states the scale AND the rounding contract, and a value whose contract you cannot see is
// exactly what this language exists to make visible. It is also where an inferred type lives
// now — `let x = $19.99;` did not delete the type, it moved it into the editor.
//
// **The smallest expression under the cursor wins**, because expressions nest: in `price * qty`
// the cursor on `qty` must say `Int`, not the type of the product. `lsp.rs` gets that by comparing
// span WIDTHS. A Burxt `Node` carries one token index and no end offset, so there are no widths to
// compare — and the SELECTION needed none. A token is the smallest thing a cursor can be inside,
// so: find the token under the cursor, then the node that STARTS at it. Several nodes can start at
// one token, the arena is built bottom-up, and the operand is therefore always the lower index —
// so the smallest expression is the first node found. That is the same rule read off the arena's
// construction order rather than off a field.
//
// **What the missing end offset does cost is the RANGE, and here is the measurement.** Driven
// against `burxt lsp` at every one of 67,663 cursor positions across 41 files:
//
//   both answer a hover                       6,963
//     identical CONTENTS (the type and note)   6,902   — 99.1%
//     identical RANGE                          4,072   — 58%
//   only the Rust server answers              2,870   — 4.2% of all positions
//   only this server answers                  1,286
//
// Two differences, both structural and neither hidden:
//
//   * **The range is the TOKEN, not the expression.** Hovering on `s` in `len(s)` gives the same
//     range on both sides; hovering on `len` gives `len` here and `len(s)` there. For a leaf —
//     which is what a person hovers on — they agree, which is why the 58% is not the number that
//     matters and the 99.1% is.
//   * **This server answers only INSIDE a token that begins a typed node.** The 2,870 are almost
//     all `=`, `(`, `)`, `,`, `.`, `:`, `;` and the space between tokens: a nesting span covers
//     those and a token does not. Closing it needs a start AND an end on `Node`, which is a change
//     to `ast.bx` and is reported rather than guessed at — a fallback to "the nearest earlier
//     token that starts something" would answer confidently and often wrongly, which is worse in
//     an editor than answering nothing.
//
// The 1,286 the other way are positions inside string literals and a few identifiers where
// `expr_types` on the Rust side has no entry. Not chased further: this server answering more is
// not a defect, and chasing it would be chasing the other compiler's cache.
function (self: Unit) lsp_token_under(offset: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(self.tokens) {
        let t: Token = self.tokens[i];
        if t.start <= offset && offset < t.start + t.length {
            return i;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

function (self: Unit) lsp_node_at_token(token: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(self.typed) {
        if self.nodes[i].token == token && !is_unknown(self.typed[i]) {
            return i;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// One sentence about what a type GUARANTEES, where the name does not already say it. The part
// worth hovering for: a scale is visible in the type, but what happens to a result that does
// not fit that scale is the whole question. Word for word `lsp.rs`'s `explain`, because two
// wordings for one guarantee is how a language starts feeling arbitrary.
function (self: Unit) lsp_explain(src: String, t: Type) -> String {
    if t.kind == 45 {
        let mutable places: String = " decimal places";
        if t.scale == 1 {
            places = " decimal place";
        }
        if t.contract == 1 {
            return "Exact decimal, " + to_string(t.scale) + places
                    + ". A result that needs rounding rounds half to even (banker's rounding).";
        }
        if t.contract == 2 {
            return "Exact decimal, " + to_string(t.scale) + places
                    + ". A result that needs rounding rounds half away from zero.";
        }
        return "Exact decimal, " + to_string(t.scale) + places
                + " — no rounding contract, so any operation that could round is a compile error "
                + "until one is declared.";
    }
    if t.kind == 43 {
        return "C's 32-bit `int`, at the FFI boundary only.";
    }
    if t.kind == 51 {
        return "An opaque pointer from C. Only `c_is_null(p)` and `c_string_at(p)` may look at it.";
    }
    if t.kind == 44 {
        return "C's `double`. A Decimal may not cross as one — it would lose exactness.";
    }
    if t.kind == 49 {
        return "A interface object: dispatch to whichever type implements `"
                + substring(src, t.name_start, t.name_length) + "`, decided at runtime.";
    }
    return "";
}

// The type under the cursor, as a `hover` result, or `null`.
//
// The whole front end runs, and its complaints are captured and thrown away: hover must work
// in a file that does NOT compile — above and below the mistake — because the checker recovers
// per statement instead of stopping. That is the same reason `lsp.rs` ignores the checker's
// result here and reads the type cache anyway.
function lsp_hover(uri: String, buffer: String, line: Int, character: Int) -> String touches files {
    // The same splice as `lsp_diagnostics`: hover inside `main.bx` must see the types its `use`
    // lines bring in, or every one of them reads as unknown and the answer is `null`.
    let prefix: String = lsp_program_prefix(uri, buffer);
    let shift: Int = len(prefix);
    let src: String = prefix + blank_imports(buffer);
    let offset: Int = shift + lsp_offset_of(buffer, line, character);
    let mutable unit: Unit = Unit { pending_public: 0, package_names: [], package_starts: [], package_ends: [], private_names: [], private_owners: [], tokens: [], keywords: [], nodes: [], children: [], nested_children: [], items: [], bindings: [], consts: [], functions: [], stack: [], methods: [], types: [], params_in_scope: [], generics: [], type_parameters: [], type_param_bounds: [], pending_bounds: [], current_generic: 0 - 1, current_method: 0 - 1, probing: 0, probe_changes: 0, grow_parameters: [], grow_self: [], relay_parameters: [], relay_self: [], relay_method_parameters: [], relay_aliases: [], relay_ceiling: 0, type_bindings: [], measure_clause: 0 - 1, measure_slot: 0, measure_parameters: 0 - 1, measure_owner: "", old_nodes: [], old_slots: [], ensures_start: 0, ensures_count: 0, ensures_owner: "", bracket_clauses: [], clause_subjects: [], it_token: 0 - 1, it_is_result: 0, used_it: 0, it_seen: 0, result_type_node: 0 - 1, result_slot: 0 - 1, returns_node: 0 - 1, walked_types: [], type_binding_floor: 0, binding_floor: 0, allowed_effects: 63, effects_owner: "", function_buckets: [], function_chain: [], instances: [], instance_arguments: [], impls: [], impl_traits: [], typed: [], region_depth: 0, region_token: 0 - 1, in_caller_region: 0, in_pure: 0, loop_depth: 0, in_contract: 0, in_ensures: 0, current_function: -1, chunks: [], pending: "", globals: "", global_chunks: [], sret: "", sret_cells: 0, function_entry: "", body_chunks: [], body_pending: "", next_register: 0, next_slot: 0, next_label: 0, slots: [], strings: [], declared: [], loop_condition_labels: [], loop_end_labels: [], region_marks: [], loop_region_depths: [], block_release: [], placing: 0, rel_frames: [], rel_names: [], in_function: 0, depth: 0, param_depth: 0 - 1, problems: 0, diagnose: 0, problem_start: 0 - 1, problem_length: 0, problem_message: "", position: 0, token_end: 0, no_record_literal: 0, in_extern_signature: 0, range_bounds: 0, in_multiply: 0, print_to_stderr: 0, int_type_node: 0 - 1, errors: 0, type_of_calls: 0, find_binding_calls: 0, find_function_calls: 0, show_type_calls: 0, expr_allocates_calls: 0, type_at_calls: 0 };
    unit.install_keywords();

    let path: String = lsp_scratch(".hover");
    let saved: Int = lsp_capture_begin(path);
    // ---- nothing from here to `lsp_capture_end` may return or exit ----
    unit.run(src);
    let mutable parsing: Bool = true;
    while parsing {
        if unit.done() {
            parsing = false;
        } else {
            let item: Int = unit.parse_item(src);
            if item < 0 {
                parsing = false;
            } else {
                push(unit.items, item);
            }
        }
    }
    if unit.errors == 0 {
        unit.check(src);
    }
    lsp_capture_end(saved);
    // ---- the capture window ends here ----

    let token: Int = unit.lsp_token_under(offset);
    if token < 0 {
        return "null";                               // whitespace: nothing, rather than a guess
    }
    let node: Int = unit.lsp_node_at_token(token);
    if node < 0 {
        return "null";
    }
    let shown: String = unit.show_type(src, unit.typed[node]);
    let note: String = unit.lsp_explain(src, unit.typed[node]);
    let mutable value: String = "```burxt\n" + shown + "\n```";
    if len(note) > 0 {
        value += "\n" + note;
    }
    let t: Token = unit.tokens[token];
    if t.start < shift {
        return "null";                               // the cursor landed in a sibling's text
    }
    return "\{\"contents\":\{\"kind\":\"markdown\",\"value\":" + json_escape(value)
            + "\},\"range\":" + lsp_range(blank_imports(buffer), t.start - shift,
            t.start + t.length - shift) + "\}";
}

// ---- documents -------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// uri -> the client's current text for it. **The buffer, not the file**: diagnostics run on the
// client's in-memory text, so they are right while the file on disk is stale, which is the
// entire point of an editor integration.
//
// **On disk, and this is the design decision in this file that a reviewer should push back on
// hardest, so here is the measurement that forced it.** `lsp.rs` keeps a `HashMap<String,
// String>`. The first version here kept two parallel arrays, which meant the store — and
// therefore the message it was filled from — had to be allocated OUTSIDE the per-turn region,
// since a value that crosses out of a closing region is a dangling pointer. Measured on
// `examples/tour.bx` (2.9 KB): 140 KB of region per keystroke that is never given back, or
// about 7000 keystrokes before *"region memory exhausted"*. Proportional to the file, so a
// 30 KB module gets about 700 — ten minutes of typing.
//
// Nested regions are not available and a value cannot be moved between regions, so there is no
// way to recycle an in-memory store. The filesystem is the store that outlives a region. With
// it, **every allocation a turn makes is inside the turn**, and memory is flat for as long as
// the editor is open — measured below.
//
// The path is derived from the uri with `hash`, so no in-memory map is needed either. A file
// holds `uri\ntext`, and the uri is checked on the way out: two open documents whose uris hash
// alike would otherwise serve each other's text, and answering "not open" is the safe end of
// that. Rare, and named rather than assumed away.
function lsp_doc_path(uri: String) -> String {
    let pid: Int = getpid();
    let mutable key: Int = hash(uri);
    if key < 0 {
        key = 0 - key;
    }
    return "/tmp/burxt-lsp-" + to_string(pid) + "-" + to_string(key) + ".buf";
}

function lsp_doc_store(uri: String, text: String) -> Int touches files {
    write_file(lsp_doc_path(uri), uri + "\n" + text);
    return 0;
}

// The stored text for `uri`, or `""` when it is not open. `""` is also what an empty buffer
// answers, and the two really are the same here: there is nothing to check either way.
function lsp_doc_text(uri: String) -> String touches files {
    let path: String = lsp_doc_path(uri);
    if !lsp_readable(path) {
        return "";
    }
    let held: String = read_file(path);
    let stop: Int = line_end_at(held, 0);
    if stop != len(uri) || substring(held, 0, stop) != uri {
        return "";                                   // a different document hashed here
    }
    return substring(held, stop + 1, len(held) - stop - 1);
}

function lsp_doc_open(uri: String) -> Bool touches files {
    return len(lsp_doc_text(uri)) > 0;
}

// Closing writes an empty file rather than removing one: `remove` would be another extern, and
// an empty file fails the uri check above, which is exactly "not open".
function lsp_doc_forget(uri: String) -> Int touches files {
    write_file(lsp_doc_path(uri), "");
    return 0;
}

// ---- serve -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **The fourth wall, and the measurement that dissolved it — then the measurement that showed
// the first one had not.** A language server runs for hours and re-checks the buffer on every
// keystroke; a region is reclaimed when it CLOSES, so a server whose whole life is one region
// allocates a compiler's working set per keystroke and never gives it back. 1 GB per process is
// the reservation, which makes that a countdown rather than a design.
//
// A `region` opened per turn INSIDE the loop is reclaimed per turn: 2000 turns each allocating
// 6.4 MB peaked at 4.5 MB resident. That was measured first and it was not enough, because the
// first version put only the CHECK inside the turn and left the message and the document store
// outside it — they have to survive into the next turn, and a value cannot cross out of a
// closing region. 200 keystrokes on `examples/tour.bx` peaked at 756 MB and the server died at
// about 250. Fixing `json_string_bytes` (see there) took it to 140 KB a turn, which is 7000
// keystrokes: better, and still a countdown.
//
// So **the whole turn is inside the region** — reading the frame, scanning it, checking, and
// writing the reply — and the store that has to outlive it is the filesystem. What crosses the
// boundary is three Ints, and an Int is a copy rather than a pointer.
//
// That last point is not a style preference. A top-level mutable assigned a STRING from inside a
// region compiles today without complaint and then reads back as whatever the next allocation
// put there — a silent use-after-free, reproduced in five lines. It is a compiler bug and it is
// reported separately; this loop is written so that it would be correct either way.
function lsp_serve() -> Int touches input, files {
    let mutable serving: Int = 1;
    let mutable shutting_down: Int = 0;
    let mutable clean: Int = 0;

    while serving == 1 {
        // ---- one turn, one region ----
        region turn {
            let frame: [Int] = lsp_read_frame();
            if len(frame) == 0 {
                serving = 0;                         // stdin closed: the client is gone
            } else {
                let message: String = lsp_bytes_as_text(frame);
                let method: String = json_string_at(message, json_member(message, 0, "method"));
                // A request has an id and must be answered; a notification has none and must not
                // be. Kept VERBATIM so a string id is echoed as a string.
                let id: String = json_raw_at(message, json_member(message, 0, "id"));
                let uri: String = json_string_at(message,
                        json_member3(message, 0, "params", "textDocument", "uri"));

                if method == "initialize" {
                    if len(id) > 0 {
                        lsp_respond(id, lsp_initialize_result());
                    }
                } else if method == "initialized" {
                    clean += 0;                      // nothing to do, and not an error either
                } else if method == "textDocument/didOpen" {
                    let text: String = json_string_at(message,
                            json_member3(message, 0, "params", "textDocument", "text"));
                    lsp_doc_store(uri, text);
                    lsp_publish_message(uri, lsp_diagnostics(uri, text));
                } else if method == "textDocument/didChange" {
                    // Full sync: the LAST change carries the whole document.
                    let changes: Int = json_member2(message, 0, "params", "contentChanges");
                    let last: Int = json_array_last(message, changes);
                    let text: String = json_string_at(message, json_member(message, last, "text"));
                    lsp_doc_store(uri, text);
                    lsp_publish_message(uri, lsp_diagnostics(uri, text));
                } else if method == "textDocument/didSave" {
                    // The buffer is already authoritative, so a save changes nothing the server
                    // knows. Republished anyway, because a client may have dropped diagnostics
                    // in between.
                    lsp_publish_message(uri, lsp_diagnostics(uri, lsp_doc_text(uri)));
                } else if method == "textDocument/didClose" {
                    lsp_doc_forget(uri);
                    // Clear the squiggles for a file no longer open, or the client keeps showing
                    // errors for a buffer that is gone.
                    lsp_publish_message(uri, "[]");
                } else if method == "textDocument/hover" {
                    if len(id) > 0 {
                        let line: Int = json_int_at(message,
                                json_member3(message, 0, "params", "position", "line"));
                        let character: Int = json_int_at(message,
                                json_member3(message, 0, "params", "position", "character"));
                        let text: String = lsp_doc_text(uri);
                        if len(text) == 0 || line < 0 || character < 0 {
                            lsp_respond(id, "null");
                        } else {
                            lsp_respond(id, lsp_hover(uri, text, line, character));
                        }
                    }
                } else if method == "shutdown" {
                    shutting_down = 1;
                    if len(id) > 0 {
                        lsp_respond(id, "null");
                    }
                } else if method == "exit" {
                    // Per the protocol: a clean exit after shutdown, an error otherwise.
                    serving = 0;
                    if shutting_down == 0 {
                        clean = 1;
                    }
                } else if len(id) > 0 {
                    lsp_method_not_found(id, method);
                }
                // A message that parses to no method at all falls through here and is ignored.
                // **No panics on bad input**: a malformed message is answered or dropped, never
                // fatal, because a language server that dies takes the editor's language support
                // with it until a restart.
            }
        }
    }
    return clean;
}
